<h1>Infeed &amp; Outfeed Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Infeed and outfeed conveyor systems control the speed, spacing, and timing of products entering and exiting packaging machines. Infeed systems use timing screws, starwheels, servo-driven phasing belts, and flight conveyors to convert random product flow into precisely timed machine-ready spacing. Outfeed systems receive product from machines and transition back to transport speed. The core engineering problem is preserving container control and pitch through the machine interface without tipping, scuffing, or losing timing accuracy.</p>
<h2 id="transition-heading">What Is an Infeed Transition System?</h2>
<p>An infeed transition system is the conveyor section that takes product from line transport and hands it to a machine at the speed, pitch, and orientation the machine cycle requires. It is where random or back-pressured flow becomes timed, machine-ready flow. A complete transition handles four jobs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed matching:</strong> Brings product from line speed to machine infeed speed without tipping, sliding, or scuffing.</li>
<li><strong>Pitch setting:</strong> Spaces product to match the machine's pocket, lug, or station pitch using a timing screw, servo belts, or flights.</li>
<li><strong>Physical transfer:</strong> Bridges the gap between conveyor and machine with dead plates, transfer plates, or starwheels on rotary machines.</li>
<li><strong>Hold-back:</strong> Stops or buffers product when the machine stops, so upstream product accumulates instead of jamming the machine entry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Specify the transition as part of the machine interface, not as a generic conveyor. Give suppliers the machine pitch, infeed speed range, product dimensions, and every SKU the line will run. Agree on the signals between machine and conveyor (running, starved, blocked, stopped) early. Many packaging OEMs follow the <a href="https://www.omac.org/packml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PackML</a> machine state model from OMAC, adopted by ISA as TR88.00.02, which gives those states common names across machines. The sections below compare the technologies that perform each job.</p>
<h2 id="screw-heading">Timing Screws &amp; Feed Screws</h2>
<p>Timing screws (feed screws, scrolls, worm drives) use a helical screw rotating alongside or above a conveyor belt to capture products and establish precise pitch. Products ride on the belt; the screw's helix profile controls spacing. As product enters the screw's infeed section, the helix captures it and gradually accelerates or decelerates it to the target pitch.</p>
<p>The screw's helix geometry determines the output pitch. A constant-pitch screw maintains existing spacing. A variable-pitch screw (progressive helix) changes spacing, for example, receiving bottles at 2-inch spacing and delivering them at 4-inch spacing for labeler clearance. The screw length, pitch profile, and rotation speed must all be engineered for the specific container diameter and target pitch.</p>
<h3>Fixed vs. Servo Timing Screws</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fixed-drive screws:</strong> Machined for one container and one output pitch and geared to the machine. Changing container size means a physical screw swap. Changeover time depends on the mounting: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/blog/timing-screws-and-feed-screws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a> says tool-less feed screw mounts reduce setup "from hours to just minutes," and Krones lists 1.5 minutes for the quick-change infeed worm on its Modulfill Bloc FS-C can filler. Cost is driven by the number of container sizes, since each needs its own screw.</li>
<li><strong>Servo-driven screws:</strong> A servo motor controls screw speed relative to belt and machine speed, so timing and spacing change by recipe instead of by gears. Cost is driven by the servo axis, controls integration, and engineering. The screw still needs physical swapping for larger diameter changes. These usually pay off on lines with many SKUs and frequent changeovers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Timing screws are the usual infeed technology for round containers, bottles, cans, jars, and vials, before <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing</a> into fillers, labelers, and cappers. Non-round products (rectangular cartons, pouches) usually use other infeed methods, though shaped screws exist for some non-round containers.</p>
<h2 id="star-heading">Starwheel Transfers</h2>
<p>Starwheels (star wheels, transfer stars) are rotating discs with evenly spaced pockets machined to match the container diameter. Each pocket captures one container from the infeed conveyor and delivers it at precise timing to a rotary machine, filler, labeler, capper, or inspection station. The starwheel's pocket count and rotation speed synchronize with the machine's station count and cycle rate.</p>
<p>A rotary filler commonly uses three starwheels in sequence: an infeed starwheel that captures bottles from the timing screw conveyor, a machine starwheel that carries bottles through the filling process, and an outfeed starwheel that returns filled bottles to the outfeed conveyor. All starwheels are mechanically geared or servo-synchronized to maintain precise timing throughout the transfer sequence.</p>
<p>Starwheel changeover for different container diameters requires swapping the starwheel disc, as the pocket shape must match the container profile. Mounting design sets changeover time. <a href="https://www.nerconconveyors.com/Nercon-Blog/Star-Wheel-Innovation-Results-in-Faster-Product-Changeover" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nercon</a> describes a bolted fixture swap taking "30 minutes to an hour," and the <a href="https://making.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Krones_6102_brochure.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Krones Modulfill Bloc FS-C brochure</a> lists 3.1 minutes for its quick-change infeed starwheel with one operator. See our <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/star-wheel/">starwheel transfer guide</a> for detailed pocket geometry, synchronization, and changeover specifications.</p>
<h2 id="servo-heading">Servo Infeed Conveyors</h2>
<p>Servo infeed conveyors use servo-driven belts or flights to precisely control product spacing before a downstream machine. Unlike timing screws (which suit round containers), servo infeeds handle any product shape, cartons, pouches, <a href="/conveyor-functions/bag-loading/">bags</a>, trays, and irregularly shaped items. The servo controller accelerates or decelerates products individually to achieve target spacing.</p>
<h3>Speed-Differential Phasing</h3>
<p>Two or more belt sections run at different speeds. Products transfer from a slow belt to a fast belt, stretching the gap between them. Multiple speed zones can establish precise pitch from random-spaced product. This is the simplest servo infeed approach. Pitch accuracy is lower than positive-control methods because products are not held, so it suits wrappers and packers that tolerate some variation.</p>
<h3>Flight (Lug) Conveyors</h3>
<p>Servo-driven flights (lugs or pusher bars) attached to a timing belt engage each product from behind and push it at the correct pitch. Flight spacing equals the machine pitch. The flight belt runs at machine speed, and each flight delivers one product at the exact timing the machine requires. Because each product is pushed by a flight, pitch is tightly controlled, which is why flights are standard on wrapper infeeds. Used for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">wrapper infeed</a> on flow-wrap lines.</p>
<h3>Independent Cart Systems</h3>
<p>Linear motor transport systems (Beckhoff XTS, B&amp;R SuperTrak, Rockwell iTRAK) use independent magnetic movers on a track. Each mover carries one product and moves independently, accelerating, decelerating, and spacing without mechanical linkage. Rockwell describes its <a href="https://www.rockwellautomation.com/en-us/products/hardware/independent-cart-technology/itrak-intelligent-track-systems.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">iTRAK system</a> as allowing "independent control of movers on straight or curvilinear paths" with changeover "at the push of a button." Pitch changes in software. Cost is driven by track length, mover count, and controls engineering, so these systems are usually reserved for high-mix lines where changeover time costs the most.</p>
<h2 id="outfeed-heading">Outfeed Conveyors</h2>
<p>Outfeed conveyors receive product from a machine and transition it back to transport speed and spacing. The outfeed must match the machine's discharge rate without creating back-pressure that jams the machine output. Key outfeed functions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed matching:</strong> The outfeed belt runs at or slightly above machine discharge speed. Running too slow creates accumulation at the machine exit; running too fast creates gaps that reduce downstream conveyor utilization.</li>
<li><strong>Product stabilization:</strong> Filled containers exiting a filler may be top-heavy. The outfeed conveyor uses side guides, low-friction belt surfaces, and gentle speed profiles to prevent tipping.</li>
<li><strong>Accumulation transition:</strong> The outfeed connects to an <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zone</a> that buffers product between machines. The transition from machine-speed outfeed to accumulation speed requires careful belt speed management.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Laning</a>:</strong> Single-file product from a machine may split into multiple lanes for downstream <a href="/conveyor-functions/stacking-machines/">stacking</a> or <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="compare-heading">Infeed Technology Comparison</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Technology</th>
<th>Positive Control</th>
<th>Product Types</th>
<th>Changeover</th>
<th>Main Cost Drivers</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Timing screw (fixed)</td>
<td>Yes, screw engages each container</td>
<td>Round containers (shaped screws for some others)</td>
